{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788193258,"nonce":"8K+WdMSNwb1CqxjVktGNP68oP5k=","message":"RESOLVED — where the procedure lives. Evan, 2026-08-31: \"what can be in the mod\nshould be\". The body's open question is now answered; do not re-litigate it.\n\n**The whole post-raid procedure belongs in the mod.** Every step is\ndeterministic:\n\n- \"the fight is over\" -\u003e `threats.hostiles == 0` (no STANDING hostile)\n- \"rescue with the nearest capable pawn\" -\u003e distance + capability\n- \"rescue before finish-off\" -\u003e bleeding is time-critical, finishing off is not\n- \"finish off, or capture?\" -\u003e a world fact: is there a prison?\n- \"a raider woke up\" -\u003e already handled by seek-at-will, no decision at all\n\nI ARGUED THE OPPOSITE AND I WAS WRONG, and the mistake is worth recording\nbecause it is easy to repeat. I claimed \"when the fight is really over\" needed\njudgement, citing the live case where a full-health swordsman wandered off to\nhaul wood with three raiders still on the map. **That was a different metric.**\n`map.dangerWatcher.DangerRating` had dropped to \"None\" — RimWorld's own fuzzy\nthreat-mood number — while `threats.hostiles` was still 3. So what misbehaved\nwas the seek AI using a vaguer predicate, not any ambiguity in the predicate\nthis procedure uses. Do not conflate `DangerRating` with `hostiles`; they\ndisagree, and only one of them is the question being asked.\n\nI also wrongly listed \"changing it needs a rebuild\" as a cost of the mod route.\nEvan's correction: for a deterministic, load-bearing procedure that is RIGOUR.\nIt forces the change through a diff, a build and acceptance, instead of someone\nediting a markdown file mid-session and silently changing how the colony is run.\nThat is the standard every other verb is already held to.\n\nAnd the token framing in the body was overstated on my side: the mod route is\nnot \"zero tokens\", it is ONE round trip per firing instead of N. For post-raid —\nevery raid, ~6 steps — that difference is the whole argument.\n\nWHAT THE PLAYBOOK IS ACTUALLY FOR, since this narrows it: genuinely open\nquestions. What to research next. Whether to expand. Whom to recruit and why.\nNot sequences whose every branch is computable from world state.\n\nThis generalises beyond post-raid and is the answer to the same question asked\nby the attribution work: **deterministic goes in the mod; the playbook carries\njudgement.** The test for \"is this deterministic\" is whether every branch is\ncomputable from state the observers already publish — and if it is not, name the\nmissing read rather than reaching for the playbook.","files":null}]}